</div>
'''

# Section 4: Update Freshness
html += f'''
<div class="section" id="update-freshness">
    <h2>4. Retailer Reporting Freshness Analysis</h2>

    <p>The lag between <code>source_updated_at</code> (when the retailer last confirmed or changed the price) and <code>recorded_at</code> (when the data aggregator captured it) is a proxy for data freshness. Long lags may indicate infrequent retailer reporting, reducing the reliability of price comparisons for consumers and regulators.</p>

    <div class="kpi-row">
        {kpi_card(f'{lag_stats_full["50%"]:.1f}h', 'Median Update Lag', '#203a43')}
        {kpi_card(f'{lag_stats_full["mean"]:.1f}h', 'Mean Update Lag', '#2166ac')}
        {kpi_card(f'{lag_stats_full["90%"]:.1f}h', '90th Percentile Lag', '#2c5364')}
        {kpi_card(f'{lag_7d_pct:.1f}%', 'Records With Lag > 7 Days', '#e67e22' if lag_7d_pct > 10 else '#4a6fa5')}
        {kpi_card(f'{lag_14d_pct:.1f}%', 'Records With Lag > 14 Days', '#c0392b' if lag_14d_pct > 5 else '#4a6fa5')}
    </div>

    <img class="chart-img" src="{charts['lag_hist']}" alt="Update Lag Histogram">

    <p>The distribution of update lags is <strong>right-skewed</strong>: most records are updated within hours, but a long tail extends to weeks or more. The median lag of {lag_stats_full["50%"]:.1f} hours suggests that the typical price record is reasonably fresh, but the elevated mean ({lag_stats_full["mean"]:.1f} hours) confirms a subset of stations with very stale data.</p>

    <img class="chart-img" src="{charts['lag_by_fuel']}" alt="Update Lag by Fuel Type">

    <h3>Lag Statistics by Fuel Type</h3>
    <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Median Lag (h)</th><th>Mean Lag (h)</th><th>90th %ile (h)</th><th>Max Lag (h)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
'''

for _, row in lag_by_fuel_stats.iterrows():
    html += f'''<tr>
        <td style="font-weight:600;">{row['fuel_type']}</td>
        <td>{row['median']:.1f}</td>
        <td>{row['mean']:.1f}</td>
        <td>{row['p90']:.1f}</td>
        <td>{row['max']:.1f}</td>
    </tr>'''

html += f'''</tbody></table>

    <img class="chart-img" src="{charts['scatter']}" alt="Price vs Update Lag">

    <div class="callout warning">
        <strong>Data quality concern:</strong> {lag_14d_pct:.1f}% of records have an update lag exceeding 14 days. These stale records undermine the utility of the government's open price data for real-time consumer decision-making. Regulators should consider setting a maximum reporting interval (e.g., 48 hours) as a condition of the mandated data scheme.
    </div>
</div>
'''

# Section 5: Station Coverage
html += f'''
<div class="section" id="station-coverage">
    <h2>5. Station Coverage &amp; Fuel Type Availability</h2>

    <p>Not all UK forecourts sell every fuel type. This section examines how many fuel types each station reports and identifies gaps in coverage across the mandated categories.</p>

    <img class="chart-img" src="{charts['coverage']}" alt="Fuel Types per Station">

    <p>Of the {unique_stations:,} distinct forecourts in the dataset:</p>
    <ul style="font-size:13px; margin: 8px 0 16px 20px; color: #3a3a4a;">
'''

for idx, val in coverage_counts.items():
    pct = val / unique_stations * 100
    html += f'<li><strong>{int(val):,} stations</strong> ({pct:.1f}%) report {idx} fuel type{"s" if idx > 1 else ""}</li>'

html += f'''</ul>

    <h3>Station Count by Fuel Type</h3>
    <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Stations Reporting</th><th>% of All Stations</th><th>Coverage Assessment</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
'''

for _, row in stations_per_fuel.iterrows():
    pct = row['station_count'] / unique_stations * 100
    if pct > 80:
        assess = 'Near-universal — available at most forecourts'
    elif pct > 50:
        assess = 'Broad — available at a majority of forecourts'
    elif pct > 20:
        assess = 'Moderate — available at a significant minority'
    else:
        assess = 'Limited — niche or specialty product'
    html += f'''<tr>
        <td style="font-weight:600;">{row['fuel_type']}</td>
        <td>{row['station_count']:,}</td>
        <td>{pct:.1f}%</td>
        <td style="text-align:left; font-size:12px;">{assess}</td>
    </tr>'''

html += '''</tbody></table>

    <img class="chart-img" src="''' + charts['daily_records'] + '''" alt="Daily Record Volume">

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Coverage implication:</strong> The fact that some fuel types (particularly premium grades) are only reported by a subset of stations means price comparisons for those fuels are based on a smaller sample. Policy analysts should note this when interpreting premium-fuel price statistics — the apparent spread may partly reflect a smaller, self-selected set of reporting stations.
    </div>
</div>
'''

# Section 6: Key Findings
html += '''
<div class="section" id="key-findings">
    <h2>6. Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>

    <p>The following findings synthesise the analytical results and their relevance to fuel pricing policy, consumer protection, and market transparency.</p>

    <ol class="findings-list">
'''

# Dynamically build findings from data
findings = []

# Finding 1: Price hierarchy
cheapest = price_stats.loc[price_stats['mean'].idxmin()]
most_expensive = price_stats.loc[price_stats['mean'].idxmax()]
findings.append(
    f'<strong>Clear fuel-type price hierarchy, but significant within-type dispersion.</strong> '
    f'{cheapest["fuel_type"]} is the cheapest fuel (mean {cheapest["mean"]:.1f} ppl), while {most_expensive["fuel_type"]} is the most expensive '
    f'(mean {most_expensive["mean"]:.1f} ppl) — a premium of {most_expensive["mean"] - cheapest["mean"]:.1f} ppl. '
    f'However, prices within each fuel type vary substantially: standard deviation ranges from '
    f'{price_stats["std"].min():.1f} to {price_stats["std"].max():.1f} ppl, meaning two consumers buying the same fuel on the same day can pay meaningfully different prices. '
    f'This underscores the value of price transparency tools for consumer decision-making.'
)

# Finding 2: Market spread
highest_range = price_stats.loc[price_stats['range'].idxmax()]
highest_iqr = price_stats.loc[price_stats['iqr'].idxmax()]
findings.append(
    f'<strong>Price spreads warrant regulatory attention.</strong> The max−min range reaches {highest_range["range"]:.1f} ppl for {highest_range["fuel_type"]} '
    f'— meaning the most expensive station charges approximately £{(highest_range["range"]/100):.2f} more per litre than the cheapest. '
    f'Even the more conservative IQR (representing the middle 50% of stations) reaches {highest_iqr["iqr"]:.1f} ppl for {highest_iqr["fuel_type"]}. '
    f'For a typical 55-litre fill-up, this translates to a potential saving of £{(highest_iqr["iqr"]*55/100):.2f} simply by choosing a median-priced station over a 75th-percentile one. '
    f'Policy interventions should focus on ensuring consumers have ready access to real-time price comparison tools.'
)

# Finding 3: Update freshness
median_lag = lag_stats_full['50%']
findings.append(
    f'<strong>Retailer reporting timeliness is inconsistent.</strong> While the median update lag of {median_lag:.1f} hours indicates that most stations report reasonably promptly, '
    f'{lag_14d_pct:.1f}% of all records show a lag exceeding 14 days — meaning the published price could be over two weeks out of date. '
    f'This undermines the government\'s open data scheme. We recommend that the Fuel Finder mandate include a <em>maximum reporting interval</em> '
    f'(e.g., retailers must update prices at least every 48 hours) and that non-compliant stations be flagged publicly.'
)

# Finding 4: Station coverage
max_fuel_types = int(coverage_counts.index.max())
pct_full_coverage = (fuels_per_station == max_fuel_types).mean() * 100 if max_fuel_types > 0 else 0
findings.append(
    f'<strong>Fuel type coverage is uneven across stations.</strong> Only {pct_full_coverage:.1f}% of stations report all {max_fuel_types} fuel types, '
    f'while {coverage_counts.get(1, 0)/unique_stations*100:.1f}% report only a single fuel type. This limits the comparability of price data '
    f'across fuel grades and suggests that the mandatory reporting scheme does not yet require all stations to declare prices for all fuel types they sell. '
    f'Extending the mandate to cover all dispensed fuel types at each forecourt would improve data completeness.'
)

# Finding 5: Data quality
findings.append(
    f'<strong>Data quality issues exist but are manageable.</strong> {negative_lag_count:,} records ({negative_lag_count/total_rows*100:.2f}%) exhibit negative update lags '
    f'(<code>source_updated_at</code> after <code>recorded_at</code>), suggesting timestamp inconsistencies in the collection pipeline. '
    f'While the proportion is small, regulators should investigate the root cause — clock synchronisation, batch processing artefacts, '
    f'or timestamp field handling — to ensure the integrity of the published data feed.'
)

for f_text in findings:
    html += f'<li>{f_text}</li>\n'

html += '''</ol>

    <div class="callout success" style="margin-top: 20px;">
        <strong>Overall assessment:</strong> The UK's mandatory fuel price reporting scheme provides a valuable transparency tool, but its effectiveness is limited by inconsistent retailer reporting behaviour and uneven station coverage. Strengthening reporting obligations — particularly around update frequency and fuel-type completeness — would significantly enhance the scheme's value for consumers, regulators, and market analysts.
    </div>
</div>
'''

# Footer
html += f'''
<div class="report-footer">
    <p>UK Retail Fuel Price Trend Analysis — February 2026 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Data: UK Government Fuel Finder Scheme via fuelcosts.co.uk (CC BY 4.0)</p>
    <p>Generated {datetime.now().strftime("%d %B %Y at %H:%M UTC")} &nbsp;|&nbsp; For Government Policy Analysts &amp; Energy Economists</p>
</div>

</div>
</body>
</html>'''

# Write output
output_path = '/app/output/final_report.html'
with open(output_path, 'w') as f:
    f.write(html)

print(f"\nReport written to {output_path}")
print(f"File size: {len(html):,} bytes")
print("Done.")
